so far with obi-wan kenobi there are  some worrying issues and not the typical  dumb glaring moments like with leia's  chase scene but fundamental issues with  tone direction cinematography editing  and of course writing the thing i was  worried about the most that has  essentially come true which is the show  is not committing to a darker tone the  inquisitors hunting down the jedi in  post-order 66 is one of the darkest  parts of the star wars timeline and the  show makes it feel tensionless and like  it's not a big deal overall to me this  is mainly because the writers make the  inquisitors feel like middling villains  literally their opening scene where they  discover a jedi who ends up escaping  makes them seem like buffoons who are  bad at their jobs not to mention two of  them didn't even look like they were  trying to capture him and then just  throws down a raptor and they give up  chasing him i know the grand inquisitor  had to stop the third sister but this  act alone showcases so much incompetence  between them that they seem utterly  ineffective then when reva was  terrorizing some people and restoring a  bit of menace that was missing the other  inquisitor abruptly stops her so right  away the villains don't come across as  intimidating as they should be and  that's even exemplified through the fact  that there's a character posing as a  jedi for money and clearly isn't scared  of the consequences of being caught and  then he is caught and nothing really  happens to him i know reva didn't kill  him because he technically wasn't a jedi  but the whole idea of spreading hope and  jedi still being present in the world  should at least be punishable then  looking at the tone for the rest of the  show primarily regarding obi-wan the  writers don't really focus that much  time on him and they keep cunning around  to different happier areas like with  leia instead of fully immersing us in  obi-wan's tragic story like i assume a  lot of people were expecting a  logan-esque battered down character  study to start but what we get is a  shallow version of that where we have  brief moments that dredge up the past  most of his time is just chilling at  work and there's not a lot that invokes  or allows us to open up to obi-wan to  see how he truly feels instead of more  of a character study this first episode  felt way more plot-oriented which i  think is a bad overall direction choice  to make like you have eli mcgregor who's  a great actor so give him the material  and let him go to work and let the  audience be immersed again in his  character because what we have so far  with obi-wan is pretty interesting and  most of his scenes are actually good  it's just the show overall is one step  away from being great then there's  another glaring issue that's been  bugging me since the book of boba fett  which is how these shows are being shot  firstly it doesn't feel like they're  really trying to make the footage look  that cinematic i think a good example is  the introduction of the inquisitors  where they walk out of the ship and  everything is brightly lit there's no  contrast no interesting compositional  shots and instead they mostly rely on  wide shots this feels like a lazy  introduction and the cinematography is  not aiding the villains by making them  look menacing by any means if you  contrast this to the introduction of cad  bane where it's an extreme wide shot of  an unknown figure walking towards our  protagonist while obscured by the  radiating heat is so much more  mysterious and badass not to mention the  cool close-up showing off the great  design of the character but instead what  we get in obi-wan kenobi feels way more  generic and this isn't just for one  scene the cinematography throughout the  show so far never really blows you away  or wows you for what should otherwise  have a high production value this kind  of scares me for when they get to the  bigger action scenes because if it looks  anything like the action we've seen so  far then i'm definitely  scared but hopefully they are saving the  budget for the later episodes which i  assume are going to be more expensive  and one thing that especially does not  help the cinematography is the jarring  editing looking at the rooftop chase  scene alone i'm really surprised the  director looked at the scene and thought  it was okay between rapidly cut action  that makes everything feel incoherent  and choppy cutting between so many  different subjects reaching up to six  different people and the stupid force  parkour that is shown via wide shots  that makes her look like she's jumping  around on wires looks terrible all of  this combined with the lackluster  cinematography and weird plot beats like  lay jumping off a god damn rooftop makes  this all feel cheap not to mention i'm  picking scenes that are alright and not  the atrocious chase scene with leia or  should i say scenes which let's get into  leia because it feels kind of forced to  throw a witty quippy young leia who  looks like she's seven how old are you  ten  i don't sound like girl 10. acting like  she's 15 years old into a story that  should be focused on obi-wan the second  she showed up i immediately said oh god  don't just evolve the story into another  father-like figure escorting a kid road  trip adventure which has been done to  death along with him already doing that  with the mandalorian so hopefully she  doesn't become the primary focus for the  rest of the story and if she is then i  don't know what the writers were smoking  because they could have taken the story  in infinitely more interesting  directions than babysitting a  quote-unquote child who's written by an  adult who doesn't know how children act  this is confirming what i feared a few  months ago where an article came out  saying that disney rewrote the obi-wan  series because it was too dark and  knowing this all i can think is that  they interjected a young leia to  brighten up this story which surprise  surprise is what everyone dislikes the  most about the show so far so i feel  like the future quality of the show  hinges on a combination of disney maybe  trusting the original writers enough and  them brightening up the tone too much  because it's disney and that one hand  decapitation will probably be the most  violent thing you see i know this all  sounds very grim and i'm coming across  as hating the show but i feel very  passionate about obi-wan kenobi and i  want to critique what is wrong because  this story alone has so much potential  and i'm desperately hoping that disney  doesn't ruin it or even does a mediocre  job with it so far it's not worse than  the book of boba fett but we'll see how  it goes hopefully it gets a bit better
 

